KZ2 patch 2174: emulate the RSX depth-bounds test on Metal (draw engine).

Adapted from commit 59d1533b of Erik Martins's (erikomis) ps3recomp fork, MIT; applied unchanged to this tree's draw engine and fragment-program
decompiler, with its Metal and MSL tests. RPCS3 is cited in a comment as a behaviour
reference only (clamping the bounds into [0, 1]); no RPCS3 code.
Metal has no depth-bounds test, so draws that set SET_DEPTH_BOUNDS_TEST_ENABLE reached every
pixel their geometry covers. Deferred renderers bound their light volumes and per-cascade
shadow passes with it (Killzone 2 lights its G-buffer this way). The fragment program now
loads a float copy of the depth buffer, taken before the draw, on a texture unit the
program does not sample, and discards outside [min, max]. Off by default: with it on, the menu
text, in-game text and floor textures broke on the macOS bring-up and the cause is not found yet;
PS3RECOMP_ENG_DEPTH_BOUNDS=1 turns it on. Host test: tools/runtime/test_metal_lighting.py.

Backends: the draw-engine half runs only on a backend without set_depth_bounds that has
depth_snapshot, i.e. Metal; Vulkan has set_depth_bounds and keeps it, and D3D12 does not use
the draw engine. The decompiler gains rsx_fp_apply_depth_bounds, called only from there. With
the default off it changes nothing on any backend.

